{
  "_what": "Every table in schemas app and private, with the ERD §5 family it belongs to and the §9.1 data class(es) that family carries, read from the ERD text and nowhere else (batch 170's assertion-only part, plan \"Batch 170 -- Can do now\" (c); read by the data classification probe in scripts/db/run.mjs).",
  "_rule": "No client role (anon, authenticated, PUBLIC) may hold any privilege on a table that is REFUSED, or on a column in \"columns\" whose class is one of SECRET-4, PROVIDER-3, INTERNAL-3. A table is refused when its \"class\" is one of them, or when its \"class\" is null and EVERY class in \"erd_classes\" is one of them. A table whose \"class\" is null and whose \"erd_classes\" mix refused and other classes is a FINDING and carries \"finding\": it is not classified, by guess or otherwise. A multi-class §5 row is resolved (\"resolved_by\") only where §9.1 or §9.2 names the table's own content as an example of a refused class, which can only add a refusal; and a table that no §5 row names, whose family is therefore inferred (\"named_in_family\": false), may be resolved INTO a refused class its inferred row does not carry, only where §9.1 names that table's own content as that class's example (app.billing_webhook_receipts, §9.1 \"webhook\" as PROVIDER-3: again a refusal added, never one lifted; review round C0 F5); a reading that would lift a table out of a refused class (every one of them is a migration's, not the ERD's) is left to the ERD owner.",
  "_shape": "{ \"module\", \"family\": \"the §5 row, with its line\", \"named_in_family\": \"false when §5 does not name the table\", \"family_inferred_from\": \"then: the batch that created it, by §6\", \"erd_classes\": [\"the §5 row's classes, verbatim\"], \"class\": \"the one class, or null\", \"resolved_by\": \"the §9.1/§9.2 text that resolves a multi-class row\", \"finding\": \"what the ERD leaves open about this table\" }",
  "_columns": "Column-level classes, \"<schema>.<table>.<column>\": \"<class>\". The ERD names no column of any table, so this is empty. The PROVIDER-3 and INTERNAL-3 columns batches 120 and 121 record in their own text (publish_targets.failure_class and performance_snapshots.metrics, the column 121 calls \"the payload\", inside the client SELECT; publish_jobs.provider_request_key, its failure code and the published_posts hash outside it) are the migrations' reading, listed as findings in the batch 170 draft record, not classes here.",
